Now I understand the full picture. The test creates a DM with `authorized_peers: None`, which means the self-permit stores no `authorized_peers` fact. When the authority is sent to the node, the node stores it. When a viewer sends LayerSubscribe, the node checks `authorized_peers` in the stored authority:

```rust
let is_authorized = match authorized_peers {
    None => true, // No authorized_peers fact → open grant
    Some(serde_json::Value::Null) => true, // Explicit null → open grant
    Some(serde_json::Value::Array(arr)) => {
        arr.iter().any(|v| v.as_str() == Some(peer_did))
    }
    _ => false,
};
```

`None` (no authorized_peers fact) = open grant. So both viewers get access.

When `AddLayerAccess` is called to add viewer0, the handler reads the existing self-permit, sees that `authorized_peers` is None so defaults to an empty list, merges in viewer0's DID, and re-issues the self-permit with the updated authorized peers list.

The issue is that re-subscribing is asynchronous—the node requests updated authority from the owner, which may not complete within the test's 2-second window. More critically, the test writes data before calling `AddLayerAccess`, so during the initial open-grant phase, that data syncs to the node. When viewer1's LayerSubscribe arrives during this window, they receive the data before the access restriction takes effect.

The fix is to set `authorized_peers: Some(vec![viewer0_did])` at creation time so the authority is restricted from the start, rather than trying to restrict it after data has already synced.

For the group chat app, I need to check what's missing. The permit template already has presence configured and the app initializes it, but I'm looking at the wrong directory—the test uses `sample_apps/group-chat`, not the osvauld-demos version.

